    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "Research in Sri Lanka shows that there is a strong correlation between crime, literacy and poverty. It has been reported that the frequency of those sentenced to death is higher in the lower income categories and rate of conviction is high among those with low literacy and no schooling (Ekanayake, 2018). Individuals forced into crime in Sri Lanka largely emerge from deep-seated socio-economic vulnerabilities, primarily extreme poverty, low educational attainment, and youth unemployment. Marginalised communities and individuals lured by transnational job scams are frequently coerced into illicit activities due to desperate financial need and a lack of viable economic alternatives. The social and economic drivers of this phenomenon manifest in several distinct areas.\n\nHigh youth unemployment rates and lack of formal education severely limit legitimate career avenues, creating a vacuum where criminal syndicates can exploit desperation and recruit vulnerable youth.\n\nStructural inequalities and historical discrimination leave specific demographics, such as the community in the plantation sector, disproportionately vulnerable to exploitation and informal economies.\n\nEconomic distress has led to an increase in Sri Lankans falling victim to human trafficking. Syndicates frequently lure young, educated individuals with promises of high-paying IT or customer service jobs in countries like Thailand. Once isolated abroad, victims are often forced into modern slavery and coerced into participating in international cyber-scamming operations. Post-pandemic inflation and widespread national economic challenges have driven many into illegal activities, including human smuggling and survival crimes such as petty theft or burglary."
