    "chunk_size_bytes": 1361,
    "chunk_text": "Economic distress has led to an increase in Sri Lankans falling victim to human trafficking. Syndicates frequently lure young, educated individuals with promises of high-paying IT or customer service jobs in countries like Thailand. Once isolated abroad, victims are often forced into modern slavery and coerced into participating in international cyber-scamming operations. Post-pandemic inflation and widespread national economic challenges have driven many into illegal activities, including human smuggling and survival crimes such as petty theft or burglary.\n\nIt is seen that there is a strong correlation between crime, poverty and literacy. There is also a strong association between poverty and school dropout rates. Add to this the fact that increasing wealth of the rich and decreasing wealth of the poor are concurrent realities, then the picture is complete. Obviously this is an interconnected conglomerate of problems. It has to be addressed as such. It cannot be solved piecemeal. If one starts with the problem of crime, one sees that it cannot be solved without solving the problem of poverty and education, problems in education cannot be solved without solving the economic problems. Could the economic problems be solved without dismantling the neo-liberal global economic system that perpetuates poverty and wealth accumulation by the rich?"
